Exercise 3.3

void processPayment(Payment payment) {
    try {
        cardProcessor.process(payment);
    } catch(NoCardDetailsFoundException e) {
        cashProcessor.process(payment);
    }
}

Q1. Should try/catch blocks be used as part of ‘standard’ processing? e.g. say I have a payment processing system that
    accepts cash or card, and I first attempt to process a card payment, before throwing an error and processing cash
    if no card details are found:
A1. It's not a great use here - processing different payment options should be offered, and not try the other if one
    failed.

Q2. Will this work correctly? If we know some payments will definitely be made by cash, is there a better way to handle
    this? Why?
A2. This will technically work but of course not in the best flow, since card payment will be the first option
    regardless. Only if that failed, cash payment becomes an option.

Q3. What are some potential problems with throwing errors like this? Think about: when we want to find out about errors
    / unnecessary processing etc
A3. The current implementation doesn't communicate the specific problem to the user. Did the issue happened because
    wrong card details were entered? Was it because the card was invalid/expired? No enough balance? it just moves
    to the cash options once the card payment fails.

Q4. Update the above pseudocode to remove the try/catch block and handle the card/cash options more gracefully.
    Feel free to invent some methods on Payment to help you!
A4.

void processPayment(Payment payment, String method) {

    if (method.equals("card")) {
        cardProcessor.process(payment);
    } else {
        cashProcessor.process(payment);
    }

}




